if you're a fan of big movies you've  probably got a neatly organized laundry  list of films to catch this year from  Star Wars the last Jedi to Wonder Woman  all the big flicks you won't want to  miss are committed to your memory but  with all that focus on blockbusters a  ton of amazing picks may have slipped  under your radar here are some of the  underappreciated movie gems from 2017  that you just need to add to your list  and watch as soon as possible for girl  with all the gifts the zombie genre has  been crowded for decades and in the post  Walking Dead era it's only growing more  popular it's hard to come up with a new  spin on those shambling undead tropes  but the girl with all the gifts somehow  manages to do it anyway  adapted from mr Carrie's novel the story  imagines a world in which much of the  human population has been wiped out by a  fungus porn zombie plague yes just like  in the video game The Last of Us but in  the girl with all the gifts a second  generation victims retain their human  characteristics even as they are  consumed by a hunger for flesh instead  of turning into one of these Oh No the  nightmares are gonna come back in the  girl with all the gifts which saw a  limited release in the u.s. early in the  year the surface level humanity poses a  moral conundrum for a teacher tasked  with educating the so called Hungary's  and that's compounded by the harsh  utilitarian views of the doctor in  command played by Glenn Close like the  best zombie horror of a girl with all  the gifts as underlined with  thought-provoking and existential  questions about modern society and human  nature and it's also an insane thrill  ride the red turtle sweeping and  life-affirming this film is by Studio  Ghibli yup the same studio that made my  neighbor totoro the red turtle doesn't  have a single sentence of dialogue and  the VATS gives it a pinprick intensity  that'll keep your eyes glued to the  screen the entire time the movie centers  around an unnamed man shipwrecked on a  deserted tropical island who later  encounters a giant red turtle but it's  far more than what it seems  superficially it dives deep into the  tenderness and tribulations of what it  means to be human  recounting milestones and memory mark  has set over gorgeous animated design  aesthetics and an incredible score while  our nameless  were set adrift by a storm we follow  along the moving plot an engrossing  layout until we're turned inside out at  the film's climax one that builds and  bursts without warning the red turtle is  truly an experience it's utterly  captivating for its characters clues and  cry inducing resolution an absolute must  watch the autopsy of Jane Doe starring  Brian Cox and Emile Hirsch as Tommy and  Aston Tilden  the autopsy of Jane Doe brings in  frights and freakish encounters from the  get-go and doesn't let up until viewers  are totally spooked the father-son duo  in the film have an unusual bond they  both work as coroner's while that alone  is pretty strange things get far more  sinister when the Tilden's are delivered  the corpse of an unidentified woman one  that shows increasingly strange signs of  trauma who is this woman what happened  to her is she even dead the autopsy of  Jane Doe sizzles with an easiness and  oddities with a peppering of jump-scares  and gory slashings it's all bubbles over  into a climax you won't see coming  Justice League dark you might have been  too distracted by the blockbuster Marvel  and DC releases coming this year to see  Justice League dark which is a shame the  Warner Brothers bagged animated flick  offers up a refreshing angle on the  standard superhero storylines it blends  together razor-sharp wit and a lot of  screen time for everyone's favorite  trench-coated troublemaker John  Constantine as the title implies Justice  League dark is well much darker than  your typical comic-book movie it's got a  risk er rating from the MPAA and  adventures into new territory beyond the  stock Points fans have become almost soo  familiar with it maintains a quick pace  and sets up a spine-tingling whodunit  mystery but it doesn't rest on shock for  Chuck's sake or any fluff that falls  flat though Constantine's trademark  rapid-fire swearing is absent from the  film it's one that's absolutely worth a  watch now that you know it exists you're  welcome  the law though this perfectly odd polish  flicks on international release in 2015  it finally hit the States in February  2017 and it's hard to express how great  that is for film fans a mashup of  musical camp and horrorcore sprinkled  with classic movie monsters and vampire  summer Mase the law is wicked and weird  right down to its flippy fishtail what's  the premise you asked well its plot  centers around mermaid vampires turn  strippers who attempts to lead normal  lives even under the neon lights of a  nightclub but they're first for blood  sex and violence causes rifts in their  relationships and also hurts their  chances of staying stealthy the law is  deliciously dicey with its characters  and turns on a dime of its disjointed  scenes but that's what makes it so  gripping it's no teeny bopper aquamarine  dream that's for sure maybe you could  meet me in Fiji champagne xx if there's  two things horror filmmakers love its  anthologies and possessed children this  Sundance Film Festival gem brings  together both in a blood-boiling  marriage the brainchild of a group of  female directors including Saint Vincent  the indie rock guitar goddess her real  name is Annie Clark by the way x-axis  comprised two four.hoorah mini films the  short stories weave through familiar  relations of mother and son past  animalistic behaviors and inter mental  illnesses and the deep beiongs the grit  and gore of camping trips gone wrong XX  finds its strengths and its subtle  silliness while scary enough in their  own right the short tails are intercut  with haunting stop-motion cutscenes that  bring it all together but there's still  an element of Campina stew it it just  all works a monster cause looking at the  poster for a monster calls you might be  tempted to assume it's just another  young adult movie about a kid who hangs  around with a talking tree monster and  hey we already have the guardians of the  galaxy so who needs that the reality  however is far more complex and  compelling the film which saw a limited  release in 2016 before opening wide this  year tells the story of a 12 year old  boy are struggling to cope with his  mother's terminal illness while facing  bullies at school and a prickly  grandmother at home dark and honest in a  way that family-friendly features rarely  dare to be these days a monster cause  was perhaps destined to fall through the  cracks in theaters but it received heaps  of praise from critics for a reason  don't miss it to the bone minimalistic  but moving  the bone tells a tale of illness and  isolation it follows Ellen played by  Lily Collins a headstrong young woman  who's been grappling with anorexia for  most of her life enter dr. William  Beckham played by an impeccably cast  Keanu Reeves who runs an eccentric  wellness clinic and runs ellen past her  wit's end directed by buffy the vampire  slayer' writer producer Marti Noxon to  the bone is black comedy at its best and  the most beautiful full of crackling  humour and sharp tongued sarcasm Netflix  was so enamored with Noxon directorial  debut the streaming service snatched up  the rights to it back in January for a  cool eight million dollars soon you'll  have no excuse to miss it love song so  Jung Kim's love song that weaves a warm  account of two women on the cusp of  self-discovery Mindy and her best friend  Sarah embark on an impromptu road trip  and are soon confronted with emotions  they can't deny Kim's past credits  include incredibly introspective films  such as in between days for Ellen and  treeless Mountain and she brings the  same kind of intricate and intimate 